So these peripherals

282
00:09:54,550 --> 00:09:58,630
can all affect the execution flow of our CPU.

283
00:09:58,630 --> 00:10:01,570
But as we know,

284
00:10:01,570 --> 00:10:03,170
based on what we just introduced,

285
00:10:03,170 --> 00:10:05,830
for external interrupts,

286
00:10:05,830 --> 00:10:09,270
our CPU actually only has a single input pin.

287
00:10:09,270 --> 00:10:11,990
That is to say, there are many peripherals,

288
00:10:11,990 --> 00:10:14,370
and we can't possibly take one interrupt line

289
00:10:14,370 --> 00:10:16,830
from each peripheral into the CPU,

290
00:10:16,830 --> 00:10:18,090
right?

291
00:10:18,090 --> 00:10:20,350
Besides, the set of peripherals changes often,

292
00:10:20,350 --> 00:10:21,450
and there may even be hot-plugging.

293
00:10:21,450 --> 00:10:23,370
If we brought in one pin

294
00:10:23,370 --> 00:10:24,550
for every single peripheral,

295
00:10:24,550 --> 00:10:26,870
then the CPU design would become far too complex.

296
00:10:26,870 --> 00:10:27,750
So in practice,

297
00:10:27,750 --> 00:10:29,230
our CPU has only one

298
00:10:29,230 --> 00:10:31,890
input pin for external interrupts.

299
00:10:31,890 --> 00:10:33,970
So what do we do?

300
00:10:33,970 --> 00:10:35,910
This is where a conversion comes into play.

301
00:10:35,910 --> 00:10:37,450
and aggregation is a problem

302
00:10:37,450 --> 00:10:39,630
So that's why we introduced

303
00:10:39,630 --> 00:10:41,990
a device called the PLIC.

304
00:10:41,990 --> 00:10:43,330
This device,

305
00:10:43,330 --> 00:10:45,570
you can think of this device as

306
00:10:45,570 --> 00:10:48,210
basically a Hub.

307
00:10:48,210 --> 00:10:50,370
Its full name is

308
00:10:50,370 --> 00:10:53,630
Platform Level Interruptor Controller,

309
00:10:53,630 --> 00:10:56,490
which is a platform-level interrupt controller.

310
00:10:56,490 --> 00:10:58,030
It is an interrupt controller.

311
00:10:58,030 --> 00:11:00,830
Now, why is it called Platform Level?

312
00:11:00,830 --> 00:11:02,390
We talked about Platform earlier.

313
00:11:02,390 --> 00:11:04,310
You can understand it as basically an SoC,

314
00:11:04,310 --> 00:11:04,970
right?

315
00:11:04,970 --> 00:11:08,270
So it is a type of interrupt controller that is integrated into the SoC.

316
00:11:08,270 --> 00:11:09,650
of interrupt controller.

317
00:11:09,650 --> 00:11:12,850
Of course, there are other types of interrupt controllers as well.

318
00:11:12,850 --> 00:11:15,050
What we mainly introduce here

319
00:11:15,050 --> 00:11:18,470
is the Platform-type interrupt controller.

320
00:11:18,470 --> 00:11:19,870
Now, its main function

321
00:11:19,870 --> 00:11:21,950
What this picture shows is that

322
00:11:21,950 --> 00:11:23,370
on the left side here,

323
00:11:23,370 --> 00:11:24,610
there are lots and lots of peripherals.

324
00:11:24,610 --> 00:11:25,670
Right?

325
00:11:25,670 --> 00:11:27,310
There are lots and lots of peripherals.

326
00:11:27,310 --> 00:11:28,750
So coming in from that side,

327
00:11:28,750 --> 00:11:29,470
on the left,

328
00:11:29,470 --> 00:11:31,130
you have lots and lots of pins coming in,

329
00:11:31,130 --> 00:11:32,250
like a hub,

330
00:11:32,250 --> 00:11:33,590
all hooked up together.

331
00:11:33,590 --> 00:11:34,670
But over here,

332
00:11:34,670 --> 00:11:35,350
going out,

333
00:11:35,350 --> 00:11:36,850
for a single HUT,

334
00:11:36,850 --> 00:11:37,830
there is only one pin.

335
00:11:37,830 --> 00:11:40,270
Of course, since there are quite a few HUTs on this side,

336
00:11:40,270 --> 00:11:41,490
it can,

337
00:11:41,490 --> 00:11:43,630
for each HUT,

338
00:11:43,630 --> 00:11:45,130
route a separate line out.

339
00:11:45,130 --> 00:11:46,810
But if we

340
00:11:46,810 --> 00:11:48,390
look at just one HUT,

341
00:11:48,390 --> 00:11:48,930
In fact,

342
00:11:48,930 --> 00:11:50,970
it's just like a multiplexer

343
00:11:50,970 --> 00:11:52,330
that is, there are lots and lots of lines here,

344
00:11:52,330 --> 00:11:53,270
but over here,

345
00:11:53,270 --> 00:11:54,290
only one line comes out

346
00:11:54,290 --> 00:11:56,730
So this PLIC,

347
00:11:56,730 --> 00:11:59,330
it's actually just like a multiplexer.

348
00:11:59,330 --> 00:12:00,350
It,

349
00:12:00,350 --> 00:12:01,230
on its left side,

350
00:12:01,230 --> 00:12:02,530
faces a whole bunch of external devices,

351
00:12:02,530 --> 00:12:03,010
while over here,

352
00:12:03,010 --> 00:12:03,630
there is only one line.

353
00:12:03,630 --> 00:12:05,090
So what it does in this,

354
00:12:05,090 --> 00:12:06,230
its main function,

355
00:12:06,230 --> 00:12:07,270
is really just,

356
00:12:07,270 --> 00:12:08,890
as everyone knows,

357
00:12:08,890 --> 00:12:09,990
the CPU,

358
00:12:09,990 --> 00:12:10,950
it can only,

359
00:12:10,950 --> 00:12:12,250
handle one kind of interrupt at a time

360
00:12:12,250 --> 00:12:13,890
So what it does here is

361
00:12:13,890 --> 00:12:16,510
It just acts as an agent.

362
00:12:16,510 --> 00:12:18,630
Based on the type of interrupt here,

363
00:12:18,630 --> 00:12:20,650
or some other things,

364
00:12:20,650 --> 00:12:21,710
which we will talk about,

365
00:12:21,710 --> 00:12:22,770
like its priority and so on,

366
00:12:22,770 --> 00:12:24,490
it can filter them out

367
00:12:24,490 --> 00:12:26,850
and let only one current interrupt through.

368
00:12:26,850 --> 00:12:28,390
If there are multiple interrupts

369
00:12:28,390 --> 00:12:29,230
happening at the same time,

370
00:12:29,230 --> 00:12:32,030
it will only let one interrupt through.

371
00:12:32,030 --> 00:12:32,870
And then,

372
00:12:32,870 --> 00:12:34,210
for the ones on the left,

373
00:12:34,210 --> 00:12:35,570
it coordinates all the interrupts,

374
00:12:35,570 --> 00:12:38,170
and on the right side,

375
00:12:38,170 --> 00:12:39,950
it serves one hart,

376
00:12:39,950 --> 00:12:40,470
essentially

377
00:12:40,470 --> 00:12:41,370
playing that kind of role.

378
00:12:41,370 --> 00:12:44,690
This is the interrupt controller.

379
00:12:44,690 --> 00:12:45,890
Why did we have to invent

380
00:12:45,890 --> 00:12:46,670
the interrupt controller?

381
00:12:46,670 --> 00:12:49,770
The main purpose of this kind of device

382
00:12:49,910 --> 00:12:51,070
Now, on the left,

383
00:12:51,070 --> 00:12:53,010
we have lots and lots of peripherals.

384
00:12:53,010 --> 00:12:55,530
If every peripheral has its own interrupt line,

385
00:12:55,530 --> 00:12:56,210
then this,

386
00:12:56,210 --> 00:12:56,950
in terms of terminology,

387
00:12:56,950 --> 00:12:59,810
we call it an interrupt source.

388
00:12:59,810 --> 00:13:03,190
Because all interrupts originate and come from these peripherals,

389
00:13:03,190 --> 00:13:04,230
so we call them interrupt sources.

390
00:13:04,230 --> 00:13:05,350
For every interrupt source,

391
00:13:05,350 --> 00:13:06,990
we give it a code number.

392
00:13:06,990 --> 00:13:09,010
We'll cover the related concepts later on.

393
00:13:09,010 --> 00:13:10,430
So these terms,

394
00:13:10,430 --> 00:13:11,410
I'll introduce them to you as well.

395
00:13:11,410 --> 00:13:13,630
So this is called an interrupt source.

396
00:13:13,630 --> 00:13:17,450
Now, in RISC-V,

397
00:13:17,450 --> 00:13:18,850
in its specification,

398
00:13:18,850 --> 00:13:20,230
what it specifies is that,

399
00:13:20,230 --> 00:13:22,310
the current rule is that,

400
00:13:22,310 --> 00:13:23,970
these interrupt sources

401
00:13:23,970 --> 00:13:26,710
There are 53 of them defined in total, as standard

402
00:13:26,710 --> 00:13:28,070
So its value,

403
00:13:28,070 --> 00:13:29,690
it goes from 0 to 53

404
00:13:29,690 --> 00:13:31,290
But number 0 among them,

405
00:13:31,290 --> 00:13:32,510
this interrupt source,

406
00:13:32,510 --> 00:13:34,810
it is not actually assigned to

407
00:13:34,810 --> 00:13:37,290
any specific peripheral,

408
00:13:37,290 --> 00:13:38,450
but is reserved,

409
00:13:38,450 --> 00:13:39,550
reserved and unused.

410
00:13:39,550 --> 00:13:40,990
So the actual valid range

411
00:13:40,990 --> 00:13:42,390
of interrupt sources,

412
00:13:42,390 --> 00:13:43,390
is from 1 to 53.

413
00:13:43,390 --> 00:13:45,790
Now later on,

414
00:13:45,790 --> 00:13:47,270
aren't we going to show you an example,

415
00:13:47,270 --> 00:13:48,830
the one with the serial port device?

416
00:13:48,830 --> 00:13:49,730
Well, the serial port device,

417
00:13:49,730 --> 00:13:51,370
it also has an interrupt line coming in,

418
00:13:51,370 --> 00:13:52,330
since it's a peripheral too.

419
00:13:52,330 --> 00:13:55,030
Now, this interrupt source of the serial port device,

420
00:13:55,030 --> 00:13:56,530
right here,

421
00:13:56,530 --> 00:13:57,750
We define it as a level

422
00:13:57,750 --> 00:13:59,150
Just so you're aware of it.

423
00:13:59,150 --> 00:14:00,690
This is what we call an interrupt source.

424
00:14:00,690 --> 00:14:09,030
Now, this thing, the PLIC,

425
00:14:09,030 --> 00:14:10,570
is itself a peripheral,

426
00:14:10,570 --> 00:14:11,830
it is also a peripheral, right?

427
00:14:11,830 --> 00:14:14,390
So, if we want to access,

428
00:14:14,390 --> 00:14:16,770
or rather, to operate this kind of peripheral,

429
00:14:16,770 --> 00:14:18,350
of course we also do it through its registers

430
00:14:18,350 --> 00:14:19,830
to access it.

431
00:14:19,830 --> 00:14:22,170
And this, likewise,

432
00:14:22,170 --> 00:14:24,550
is the same as the UART we talked about earlier,

433
00:14:24,550 --> 00:14:25,770
that is to say, all peripherals,

434
00:14:25,770 --> 00:14:27,950
they all use memory-mapped I/O,

435
00:14:27,950 --> 00:14:31,010
to let us access their registers.

436
00:14:31,010 --> 00:14:32,830
So, according to

437
00:14:32,830 --> 00:14:39,030
the definition in our emulator,

438
00:14:39,030 --> 00:14:42,750
the range of all of its PLIC registers,

439
00:14:42,750 --> 00:14:44,690
is specified to be within this range,

440
00:14:44,690 --> 00:14:45,570
namely 0XC

441
00:14:45,570 --> 00:14:47,690
followed by 6 zeros

442
00:14:47,690 --> 00:14:49,610
so this starts at this address

443
00:14:49,610 --> 00:14:52,950
and then there's this range of addresses after it

444
00:14:52,950 --> 00:14:55,810
used to allocate registers for it

445
00:14:55,810 --> 00:14:58,970
Now the diagram on the right

446
00:14:58,970 --> 00:15:00,030
actually shows exactly what I mean

447
00:15:00,030 --> 00:15:02,050
That is, this is basically a peripheral

448
00:15:02,050 --> 00:15:05,050
Last time we wrote UART here, right?

449
00:15:05,050 --> 00:15:07,430
Here it's the PRC

450
00:15:07,430 --> 00:15:11,330
The range of some of the PRC's registers

451
00:15:11,330 --> 00:15:13,470
is allocated within this range

452
00:15:13,470 --> 00:15:15,470
For details, you can refer to

453
00:15:15,470 --> 00:15:18,530
this code

454
00:15:18,530 --> 00:15:21,250
So its base address

455
00:15:21,250 --> 00:15:21,710
is this one

456
00:15:21,710 --> 00:15:22,370
PLC

457
00:15:22,370 --> 00:15:24,290
This just defines a base address

458
00:15:24,290 --> 00:15:28,590
So which ones are there?

459
00:15:28,590 --> 00:15:30,030
Which ones does this PLC have?

460
00:15:30,030 --> 00:15:31,990
Which interface registers does it have for programming?

461
00:15:31,990 --> 00:15:32,550
So here,

462
00:15:32,550 --> 00:15:33,810
let me first introduce to you,

463
00:15:33,810 --> 00:15:35,750
the first thing,

464
00:15:35,750 --> 00:15:37,290
as we mentioned earlier,

465
00:15:37,290 --> 00:15:37,950
this PLIC,

466
00:15:37,950 --> 00:15:39,630
one of its very important functions,

467
00:15:39,630 --> 00:15:41,350
is that based on the priority of the interrupt,

468
00:15:41,350 --> 00:15:44,050
it can select one to come through.

469
00:15:44,050 --> 00:15:45,050
So,

470
00:15:45,050 --> 00:15:47,330
we can actually handle multiple

471
00:15:47,330 --> 00:15:48,470
if there are multiple devices,

472
00:15:48,470 --> 00:15:49,410
multiple peripherals,

473
00:15:49,410 --> 00:15:52,230
then the priorities of the interrupt sources of these multiple peripherals,

474
00:15:52,230 --> 00:15:54,210
we can actually configure them individually.

475
00:15:54,210 --> 00:15:55,070
We can set them,

476
00:15:55,070 --> 00:15:56,410
and how do we set them?

477
00:15:56,410 --> 00:15:59,530
Through a register called priority, for example.

478
00:15:59,530 --> 00:16:00,990
Now this register,

479
00:16:00,990 --> 00:16:03,670
its address starts from base,

480
00:16:03,670 --> 00:16:06,110
and then based on the interrupt number times 4

481
00:16:06,110 --> 00:16:09,370
Because each register is 32 bits wide,

482
00:16:09,370 --> 00:16:10,330
which is 4 bytes.

483
00:16:10,330 --> 00:16:11,890
So you use that as the offset.

484
00:16:11,890 --> 00:16:15,350
Each PLIC interrupt source corresponds to one register.

485
00:16:15,350 --> 00:16:17,970
So if your board has,

486
00:16:17,970 --> 00:16:20,970
let's say it has fifty,

487
00:16:20,970 --> 00:16:22,690
fifty-two or fifty-three of them,

488
00:16:22,690 --> 00:16:24,050
then it's just one

489
00:16:24,050 --> 00:16:25,210
one register per interrupt source,

490
00:16:25,210 --> 00:16:26,310
that's basically the idea.

491
00:16:26,310 --> 00:16:28,330
That's one concept.

492
00:16:28,330 --> 00:16:29,250
The second one is,

493
00:16:29,250 --> 00:16:30,990
the actual value you configure,

494
00:16:30,990 --> 00:16:34,270
it's a number,

495
00:16:34,270 --> 00:16:35,590
and that number

496
00:16:35,590 --> 00:16:38,850
ranges from 0 to 7.

497
00:16:38,850 --> 00:16:40,590
Among them, 0 means

498
00:16:40,590 --> 00:16:42,590
if you set this interrupt source,

499
00:16:42,590 --> 00:16:43,510
if you configure it to 0,

500
00:16:43,510 --> 00:16:44,190
it effectively means

501
00:16:44,190 --> 00:16:45,490
this interrupt source cannot be used

502
00:16:45,490 --> 00:16:46,750
it cannot be used

503
00:16:46,750 --> 00:16:49,210
and the value goes from 1 to 7

504
00:16:49,210 --> 00:16:53,090
1 means the smaller the value, the lower it is

505
00:16:53,090 --> 00:16:54,530
1 is the lowest

506
00:16:54,530 --> 00:16:58,950
7 means this interrupt source has a higher priority

507
00:16:58,950 --> 00:17:04,690
so the PLIC, based on the priority of your interrupt source,

508
00:17:04,690 --> 00:17:05,970
it will make some decisions

509
00:17:05,970 --> 00:17:10,990
and of course, if the priority is higher,

510
00:17:10,990 --> 00:17:14,230
of course it will handle it first

511
00:17:14,230 --> 00:17:16,570
and submit it to the CPU for processing

512
00:17:16,570 --> 00:17:18,590
and you might have configured two interrupt sources

513
00:17:18,590 --> 00:17:19,990
with priorities that are the same, right?

514
00:17:19,990 --> 00:17:21,910
so if they are the same,

515
00:17:21,910 --> 00:17:25,230
according to the specification,

516
00:17:25,230 --> 00:17:27,650
it will further distinguish them by the ID value of the interrupt source

517
00:17:27,650 --> 00:17:29,710
if the ID value is smaller,

518
00:17:29,710 --> 00:17:30,850
the priority is higher

519
00:17:30,850 --> 00:17:32,110
that is how it works

520
00:17:32,110 --> 00:17:35,170
so it is essentially a two-level decision and handling process

521
00:17:35,170 --> 00:17:40,300
This is priority handling.

522
00:17:40,300 --> 00:17:43,500
Now, in our code,

523
00:17:43,500 --> 00:17:47,700
here we've also listed some of the related content in our code.

524
00:17:47,700 --> 00:17:48,900
It's actually this code.

525
00:17:48,900 --> 00:17:51,040
It's actually based on the concept we just covered.

526
00:17:51,040 --> 00:17:52,740
First, we defined a macro,

527
00:17:52,740 --> 00:17:54,360
called PIC priority,

528
00:17:54,360 --> 00:17:58,800
and its parameter is ID.

529
00:17:58,800 --> 00:18:02,420
ID is the ID value of the interrupt source,

530
00:18:02,420 --> 00:18:03,460
the number of the interrupt source.

531
00:18:03,460 --> 00:18:07,380
Then, this logic below,

532
00:18:07,380 --> 00:18:09,080
actually, this piece of code,

533
00:18:09,080 --> 00:18:10,280
what does it actually mean?

534
00:18:10,280 --> 00:18:11,600
It actually means that,

535
00:18:11,600 --> 00:18:13,760
URLQ,

536
00:18:13,760 --> 00:18:14,760
this thing is actually,

537
00:18:14,760 --> 00:18:18,540
the interrupt number of our UART device.

538
00:18:18,540 --> 00:18:19,400
This was earlier,

539
00:18:19,400 --> 00:18:20,960
it's right here, right?

540
00:18:20,960 --> 00:18:22,140
It's already here.

541
00:18:22,140 --> 00:18:23,360
I've listed them for you as well.

542
00:18:23,360 --> 00:18:26,940
So if we set it to 1,

543
00:18:26,940 --> 00:18:27,600
what does that tell us?

544
00:18:27,600 --> 00:18:32,240
It means we take this UART0 device peripheral,

545
00:18:32,240 --> 00:18:33,980
and set its priority to 1.

546
00:18:33,980 --> 00:18:36,620
And 1 is actually a relatively low priority.

547
00:18:36,620 --> 00:18:37,820
That's what it means.

548
00:18:37,820 --> 00:18:40,060
This is the priority setting.

549
00:18:40,060 --> 00:18:42,740
Now the next register,

550
00:18:42,740 --> 00:18:43,480
it's called pending.

551
00:18:43,480 --> 00:18:44,740
The pending register,

552
00:18:44,740 --> 00:18:46,760
it is actually used to... through this,

553
00:18:46,760 --> 00:18:48,260
when we read this pending register,

554
00:18:48,260 --> 00:18:53,040
we can find out whether a particular interrupt source has occurred.

555
00:18:53,040 --> 00:18:54,880
That's actually what it's used for.

556
00:18:54,880 --> 00:19:00,730
Now for this pending register,

557
00:19:00,730 --> 00:19:03,210
each PLIC actually has two of them,

558
00:19:03,210 --> 00:19:05,250
because each one is 32 bits.

559
00:19:05,250 --> 00:19:07,390
But as we know, with 32 bits,

560
00:19:07,390 --> 00:19:10,490
it can represent at most 32 interrupt sources, right?

561
00:19:10,490 --> 00:19:12,650
But as the specification says,

562
00:19:12,650 --> 00:19:15,190
there are already more than 50 of these, right?

563
00:19:15,190 --> 00:19:16,070
53 of them.

564
00:19:16,070 --> 00:19:18,290
So one register isn't enough.

565
00:19:18,290 --> 00:19:20,970
That's why it defines two registers to represent them.

566
00:19:20,970 --> 00:19:22,950
Each bit represents one.

567
